      "dom_id": "question-14005",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $10{y}^{3}-y=2{y}^{3}+y.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(y)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{y}^{3}-y & =2{y}^{3}+y \\\\ 8{y}^{3}-y & =y \\\\ 8{y}^{3}-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2y.$ We can factor out the $2y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{3}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(4{y}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{y}^{2}-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y(4{y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 2y(2y+1)(2y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $y=0,$ $z=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
