      "dom_id": "question-70466",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(t)=6{t}^{6}+15{t}^{5}+6{t}^{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(t)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n6{t}^{6}+15{t}^{5}+6{t}^{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3{t}^{4}.$ We can factor out the $3{t}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{4}(2{t}^{2}+5t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{t}^{2}+5t+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{4}(2{t}^{2}+5t+2) & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{4}(2t+1)(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0,$ $t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $t=-2.$",
